{
  "dom_id": "question-128240",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in the case when $k=4.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Earlier, we found that when $k=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x+2|+\\frac{4}{x+2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+2] \\\\  & =[2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+2] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
